##			- pr ok refuses a dirty tree or unpushed commits. gh's --delete-branch drops the branch local and remote, so anything not on origin was outside both the PR and the merge.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Regrouped the infrequent commands under nouns: newbr/gobr/listbr/land -> br create/switch/list/land, clone/connect -> repo clone/connect, pr new -> pr create. 'repository'/'branch' spell out; 'new'/'go' stay as hidden subcommand aliases.
##			- Split the old connect in two: 'repo create <owner/name>' is the only thing that will bring a remote into existence, and 'repo connect' now refuses a target that doesn't exist yet.
##			- Dropped every pre-v2 command alias (scommit, spull, scompul, saveup, spush, mkbranch, chbranch, mtm, list). v2 is a clean break, under a new name.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Dropped the bare 'commit' and 'pull' commands; update is the one command that does both. Each was a way around the workflow the tool enforces.
##			- update/sync now pull BEFORE committing. Committing first minted a local commit, so any remote that had moved ahead was diverged and the ff-only pull refused - the everyday case, and broken until now. 'pull' had been the accidental workaround.
##			- An unreachable remote warns and skips the pull instead of failing (update is the only way to commit now); --no-fetch means offline and skips the pull too. A reachable remote that can't fast-forward still fails hard.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Every command's pull honors offline now, not just update/sync's - br create/switch/land, pr ok and release called git pull directly and ignored --no-fetch.
##			- The dirty-protected-branch refusal names 'update'; it still named the dropped 'commit'. Help text back in step with the command set.
##		- 20260726 JC:
##			- Added 'br prune': deletes every local branch already merged into the merge target, plus its remote copy. Nothing else cleaned up after a PR merged elsewhere, or after an abandoned branch.
##			- Unmerged branches are kept and listed, never deleted, and there is no --force.
##			- Deletes with -D behind gitsby's own containment check: 'git branch -d' asks about the upstream, or HEAD when there is none, so it warned needlessly on one branch shape and silently refused another that was genuinely merged.
##			- A branch kept by the delete-time re-check keeps its remote copy too, and a merged current branch is reported as kept rather than left off every list.
##		- 20260727 JC: A conflicted tree is never committed. A pull whose autostash reapply conflicts exits 0, so 'git add --all' was marking the conflict resolved and committing the markers - then pushing them from sync.
##		- 20260727 JC: Review round: 'pr ok <n>' checks the PR's own branch for unpushed commits, not whichever branch you're standing on (gh force-deletes it either way); the default branch is resolved rather than assumed to be 'main', and commands refuse when it can't be told; 'release' refuses an invented version with nothing to release; 'br land' won't delete a leftover main/master; '--help' works after a command; a message may start with a dash; '--public' with '--private' is refused; credentialed URLs are masked on the execution line too; the identity probes actually cache now; 'br switch' onto the current branch stops previewing a park it doesn't do.
##		- 20260727 JC: Review round, smaller items: option and positional typos are usage errors, not crashes (no call-stack line); status and br list reject trailing arguments; 'gh pr create' is announced as it's actually run; the crash dump uses printf, so escape bytes from a filename or message stay inert; template leftovers out of the argument parser.
##		- 20260728 JC: An unreachable remote no longer fails the push. Commands that mean something locally (br create/hotfix/switch/land) skip it and say so; the ones that exist to publish (sync, pr create, pr ok, release) refuse up front instead. br land holds back the remote branch delete until the merge is published, and the hotfix back-merge falls back to the local default branch when origin's copy is the stale one.
##		- 20260728 JC: 'repo create' and 'repo connect' from a plain directory list the files they are about to publish, asked through a throwaway git dir outside the work tree so .gitignore is honored and nothing is written into the user's directory.
##		- 20260730 JC: The SSH identity line asked 'ssh -G' about the bare host, which answers with the OS login name when the target carries no user - so it named neither the connect user nor the account. It probes the connect target now, and leads with the account the key authenticates as, asked of the host rather than inferred from the key filename.
##		- 20260730 JC: Offline messages tell the truth: a park with nothing to push says so, the skipped-push warning names the branch it means (the command may move off it next), and an offline hotfix land names 'br switch <default>' then 'sync' - a bare 'sync' runs from dev after the back-merge and would have left the default branch stale on origin.
##		- 20260731 JC: Branch names display against the branch they land on ('dev :: feature/retries'); br create/hotfix name the branch they are about to make and where it comes from; the repo default gets its own line instead of a parenthetical, and br list states it before listing.
##		- 20260731 JC: The status block labels the branch you are on 'Current branch', so it reads against the 'Default branch' and 'New branch' lines beside it.
##		- 20260731 JC: br list runs in a repo whose default branch can't be told - like status it reports it unknown and lists anyway, which is what you need to see to fix it.
